-- loc.gov and Wikimedia Commons could not be opened from the newsroom sandbox on this run. Photo URLs are Wikimedia Commons original-file URLs built from file names confirmed by search (MD5 path). All eight photos are marked licensed because their license could not be confirmed; the editor should confirm each license on the file page or swap the photo.
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261128-ncpc-dec-31-meeting-trump-projects', 'national-capital-planning-commission-paoletta-december-31-meeting-trump-dc-projects-before-democrats-take-congress', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'white-house', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Trump''s planning panel chief calls New Year''s Eve meeting to push D.C. projects through before Democrats gain seats', 'Chairman Mark Paoletta told commissioners on Friday, Nov. 27, that the National Capital Planning Commission will meet on Thursday, Dec. 31, according to reporting first published by The Washington Post.', 'The White House official who chairs the federal panel that reviews construction in the nation''s capital has scheduled a special session for Thursday, Dec. 31, to move several of President Trump''s projects toward final approval, The Washington Post reported on Friday, Nov. 27. Mediaite and AlterNet carried the Post''s findings.

Mark Paoletta, chairman of the National Capital Planning Commission, informed members by email on Friday that the panel would convene on New Year''s Eve, and he also set a separate special executive session for earlier in December, according to the reports.

## What is on the table

Items expected to come up for final approval include an overhaul of the golf course at East Potomac Park, redevelopment around the Tidal Basin and West Potomac Park, and the painting of the Eisenhower Executive Office Building, Mediaite reported, citing the Post. The commission is expected to give the golf and Tidal Basin plans an initial green light at its regular meeting on Thursday, Dec. 3, the outlet said.

## Why the date matters

The commission has 12 seats. Six are held by Trump administration appointees, two by congressional Republicans and four by representatives of the District of Columbia, according to the reports. When Democrats take control of Congress in January, they inherit the two congressional seats, opening the way to 6-6 ties. A deadlocked commission cannot approve projects, Mediaite noted. The four D.C. members voted against the administration''s planned memorial arch earlier in November, the outlet said.

Rep. Jared Huffman of California, the incoming Democratic chair of the House Natural Resources Committee, called the commissioners shameless and vowed to block the projects, the Post reported.

## Courts and money

A federal judge has temporarily blocked work on the East Potomac golf course, and preservation groups and golfers are suing, according to AlterNet. Mediaite, citing The New York Times, reported that a National Park Service donations account took in $347 million in September and that the administration has not disclosed where roughly $300 million of it came from. Boston Daily could not open the Post''s article directly; the details above are as relayed by the outlets listed in the sources.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/79/Old_Executive_Office_Building_1981.jpg', 'The Eisenhower (Old) Executive Office Building in Washington, 1981 (file photo). Via Wikimedia Commons; editor to confirm the photographer and license on the file page', 'licensed', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-28.sql', 9, 1, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-28T11:00:00.000Z', '2026-11-28T11:00:00.000Z');
