INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261128-dec-11-funding-deadline-iran-war', 'congress-december-11-government-funding-deadline-stopgap-iran-war-funding-after-thanksgiving', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'congress', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'Congress returns from Thanksgiving facing a Dec. 11 funding deadline and a fight over Iran war money', 'Appropriators in both parties expect a stopgap to avoid a shutdown, with the length of that extension still unsettled, Deseret News reported.', 'Lawmakers come back from the Thanksgiving recess with federal funding set to run out after Friday, Dec. 11, and no full-year spending deal in place, according to Deseret News and ABC News.

Of the 12 annual appropriations bills, the House had passed three and the Senate none as of the week of Nov. 16, Deseret News reported on Sunday, Nov. 22. Appropriators expect a short-term extension, known as a continuing resolution, that likely runs into the new year, the outlet said.

## The sticking point

Democrats oppose providing defense money for the Iran war, and Republicans will not agree to drop it, according to Deseret News. Democrats have not shown an appetite for a shutdown over the issue, the outlet reported. ABC News reported on Monday, Nov. 9, that disputes over Iran war funding could also come to a head on the annual defense policy bill, which must pass before the end of the year.

## What appropriators said

Rep. Mike Simpson, an Idaho Republican, told Deseret News that a stopgap is needed because the bills will not be finished by Dec. 11, and that the open question is whether it runs to the end of December, the end of March or the full year. He said the issue would be taken up as soon as members returned from Thanksgiving.

Rep. Rosa DeLauro of Connecticut, the top Democrat on House Appropriations, said she did not believe anyone wanted a shutdown, and argued that one would not stop the war, according to the outlet. House Appropriations Chairman Tom Cole, an Oklahoma Republican, said in a statement that the committee should keep moving smaller packages even if they miss the deadline.

The 120th Congress, with Democrats in the majority, is seated on Sunday, Jan. 3, 2027, ABC News reported.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d9/US_House_Committee.jpg', 'A U.S. House committee hearing room (file photo). Via Wikimedia Commons; editor to confirm the photographer and license on the file page', 'licensed', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-28.sql', 8, 0, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-28T11:00:00.000Z', '2026-11-28T11:00:00.000Z');
